## 2. Task 3: Website Testing and Quality Assurance Report

### 2.1 W3C Code Validation Analysis
The website HTML5 markup and external CSS stylesheet were validated using the World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) Markup and CSS Validation Services. Initial validation identified minor non-compliant attributes, such as missing `alt` attributes on dynamic image elements and self-closing trailing slashes on void HTML5 tags (`<img>`, `<meta>`). In `style.css`, minor vendor-prefix property warnings were flagged. All missing attributes and unclosed tags were corrected, achieving 100% W3C validation compliance across all six pages.

### 2.2 Accessibility & Screen Reader Evaluation
Accessibility testing was executed using the NVDA (NonVisual Desktop Access) screen reader and browser accessibility tools to evaluate compliance for disabled users under Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG 2.1 AA):
* **Keyboard Navigation:** All interactive controls, including modal popups, drop-down menus, and form inputs, were configured with logical `tabindex` sequences and visible focus outlines.
* **Screen Reader Compatibility:** Structural tags (`<header>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`) and ARIA attributes (`aria-label`, `aria-expanded`, `aria-live`) were implemented to ensure screen readers accurately announce dynamic UI changes, such as search filtering and modal dialog visibility.
* **Color Contrast:** Text elements against dark coffee-themed backgrounds were verified to meet a minimum contrast ratio of 4.5:1.

### 2.3 Cross-Browser and Mobile Responsiveness
Testing was conducted across two distinct browser environments: Desktop Google Chrome (v122, Blink engine) and Mobile Apple Safari (iOS 17, WebKit engine).

| Test Environment | Rendering Behavior | Identified Discrepancies | Resolution Applied |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Desktop Chrome** | Full layout rendering; CSS Grid flexes seamlessly across desktop breakpoints. | CSS scrollbar transitions rendered natively without layout shift. | Standardized CSS transitions using vendor-agnostic rules. |
| **Mobile Safari (iOS)** | Responsive single-column layout; tap targets scaled for touch interaction. | Modal popup overlay permitted background scrolling on touch devices; 100vh hero section jumped during address bar collapse. | Applied `position: fixed` overflow lock to `body` on modal activation and converted height units to `svh` (small viewport height). |

### 2.4 Discrepancy Rectification & The Role of W3C
Discrepancies identified during testing—specifically touch-event scrolling conflicts, form validation focus states, and browser-specific font smoothing differences—were rectified by refining CSS property rules and event listener logic.

The World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) plays an essential role in the web ecosystem by establishing vendor-neutral technical standards and guidelines (HTML, CSS, WCAG). Adhering to W3C standards ensures interoperability across diverse web browsers, maintains forward software compatibility, guarantees digital accessibility for users with disabilities, and reduces cross-platform maintenance costs.

